==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|imperturbable}}, from {{der|en|frm|imperturbable}} and directly from {{der|en|LL.|imperturbābilis}}, from {{m|la|im-}} + {{m|la|perturbō}} + {{m|la|-bilis}}. {{surf|en|im-|perturbable}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪmpəˈtɜːbəbəl/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɪmpɚˈtɝbəbəl/|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not [[easily]] [[perturb]]ed, [[excite#Verb|excited]], or [[upset#Verb|upset]]; [[calm#Adjective|calm]] and [[collected#Adjective|collected]], even under [[pressure#Noun|pressure]]
#* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|volume=I|page=116|passage=[T]he good Dominie bore all his disasters with gravity and serenity equally '''imperturbable'''. "Prodi-gi-ous!" was the only ejaculation they ever extorted from the much-enduring man.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|volume=III|page=280|passage="We may keep it by us," replied the pawnbroker, "for months; there is no demand for such articles." "But," exclaimed she, eagerly, "I shall soon redeem it!" "So you all say," returned the man, with '''imperturbable''' coolness.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=G. Freeman Allen|title=Traffic Control on the Great Northern Line|journal=w:Modern Railways|location=Shepperton, Surrey|publisher=w:Ian Allan Publishing|month=August|year=1962|page=132|issn=0026-8356|oclc=884584817|passage=This sort of thing is meat and drink to the born Controller—and Controllers are born with the right '''imperturbable''' temperament for the job; hence the fact that they are recruited from many different grades of operating staff, and some recruits don't stay the course.}}

